t":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"00ffe75e-952c-45fc-88a9-6f1bb05adeb3","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"ca054bdb-cdaa-454f-89b4-fe1a99894c8f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a8eb74f4-7d35-4d51-ba40-5a7449d2743d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QZLoFpPgqRRRk6gnjYTZ7J","type":"tool_result","content":"=================== mcp/tools.md\n# Tools & workflows\n\n> The 103 tools and 3 ready-made workflows exposed by the PlugKit MCP server.\n\nEvery tool maps to the REST API you already know — same vocabulary, same ids.\nTools marked **write** are hidden entirely from a session granted `mcp:read`.\n\n<Note>\n  Start with `whoami`. Nearly every other tool needs a `profileId` or an\n  `accountId`, and `whoami` returns both, plus what each platform supports.\n</Note>\n\n## Search\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `search` | read | One keyword across everything the account holds: posts, conversations (by contact **and** by what was said inside them), accounts, profiles, media, comment→DM rules, sequences, broadcasts, playbooks. Returns short results with an id and a link. |\n| `fetch` | read | Read one result in full from its id — a conversation with its recent messages, a post with its per-platform targets and errors, a published post with its metrics. |\n\n<Note>\n  These two names are fixed by OpenAI: ChatGPT connectors look for `search` and\n  `fetch` to look things up and cite them. Every client gets them all the same —\n  nothing else searches inside message text.\n</Note>\n\n## Profiles\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `whoami` | read | Authenticated identity, profiles, connected accounts, and per-platform capabilities. The entry point. |\n| `list_profiles` | read | Profiles with their account and post counts. |\n| `get_profile` | read | One profile by id. |\n| `create_profile` | write | Create a profile (a workspace grouping the accounts of one brand or client). |\n| `update_profile` | write | Rename, recolour, re-describe, or set as default. |\n| `delete_profile` | write | Delete a profile **and** its accounts, posts and conversations. Irreversible. |\n\n## Accounts & connections\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_accounts` | read | Connected accounts, filterable by profile, platform or status. |\n| `get_account` | read | One account (the platform token is never exposed). |\n| `get_connect_url` | write | Generate the OAuth link for any platform. The assistant hands it to you — it can't follow it itself. |\n| `connect_telegram` | write | Attach a Telegram bot from its BotFather token (validated before storage). |\n| `connect_account_manually` | write | Attach an account from credentials you already hold. Advanced. |\n| `disconnect_account` | write | Disconnect and wipe the token. Posts and conversations are kept. |\n| `subscribe_instagram_webhooks` | write | Re-subscribe an Instagram account to Meta webhooks so DMs arrive in real time. |\n| `list_message_templates` | read | WhatsApp templates the business has had approved — the only thing that sends past the 24h window. |\n| `get_publish_options` | read | TikTok only: what that creator's account allows right now — the privacy levels it accepts, and which of comment / duet / stitch its own settings already disable. Read it before `create_post`; TikTok refuses any other value. |\n| `subscribe_facebook_webhooks` | write | Re-apply the current webhook fields to a connected Facebook Page, including `message_echoes`, which surfaces replies written from the Meta inbox. For Pages connected before those fields existed. No re-authorization needed. |\n\n## Posts\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_posts` | read | Posts with their targets and status, filterable and searchable. |\n| `get_post` | read | One post: content, status, and per target the live URL or the error. |\n| `create_post` | write | Create a post for several accounts at once — draft, scheduled (`scheduledFor`) or immediate (`publishNow`). |\n| `update_post` | write | Edit or reschedule a draft/scheduled post. Published posts are frozen. |\n| `publish_post` | write | Publish now: a draft, a scheduled post brought forward, or a retry (already-published targets are skipped, so no duplicates). |\n| `delete_post` | write | Delete on PlugKit and cancel the schedule. Does not unpublish what's already live. |\n| `unpublish_post` | write | Take a post back off the platform. The opposite of `delete_post`, which only removes it from PlugKit and leaves it live. Threads can undo a publication, Instagram and TikTok cannot. Permanent: ask first. |\n| `upload_media` | write | Re-host a file from a URL you already have, and return the PlugKit URL. |\n| `create_media_upload` | write | Get a one-shot upload link — the assistant sends the bytes itself, or hands you the browser page when it has no shell. Any size, no third-party file host. |\n| `check_media_upload` | read | Confirm the file actually landed before publishing. |\n| `list_media` | read | The account's media library — start here when a post needs a photo or a video, and pass the ids to `create_post` as `mediaIds`. |\n| `delete_media` | write | Remove a file from the library. Refused while an unpublished post still uses it. |\n| `open_media_uploader` | read | Show a drop zone in the conversation, so files on the person's machine reach you without leaving the chat. Several at once, and it stays open for more. Use it instead of `create_media_upload` when you cannot run shell commands. |\n\n<Info>\n  WhatsApp and Telegram don't do posts — only messages. `whoami` returns the\n  capability matrix so the assistant can filter targets itself.\n</Info>\n\n## Messaging\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_conversations` | read | The unified inbox across Instagram, Facebook, WhatsApp and Telegram. |\n| `get_conversation` | read | One conversation (contact, account, platform). |\n| `list_messages` | read | History of a conversation with direction and delivery status. |\n| `list_instagram_threads` | read | Raw Instagram threads with participant IGSIDs — to find a contact the inbox doesn't know yet. |\n| `send_dm` | write | DM someone by name, @handle or phone number — no conversation id. Returns the matches instead of sending when several contacts fit. |\n| `send_message` | write | Reply in an existing conversation. Immediate and irreversible. |\n| `create_conversation` | write | Open a thread with a contact identified by their platform id. |\n| `sync_conversations` | write | Pull conversations and messages from the platform API when the inbox looks stale. |\n| `send_direct_message` | write | Low-level escape hatch: send straight to a platform identifier, storing nothing in the inbox. |\n\n## Analytics\n\nReads the collected store — history, comparisons, every account at once, no\nplatform call. See [Analytics](/guides/analytics).\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_analytics_summary` | read | Headline numbers over a window, each with its change vs the previous period. Start here. |\n| `get_top_posts` | read | Posts ranked by any metric, including ones published outside PlugKit. |\n| `get_analytics_timeseries` | read | One metric by day, week or month, optionally split by platform. |\n| `get_best_times_to_post` | read | Weekday × hour engagement grid — feed it into `scheduledFor` instead of guessing. |\n| `get_follower_stats` | read | Follower history and growth per account. |\n| `get_analytics_by_account` | read | One row per connected account over the window, including the accounts that published nothing. Use it whenever two accounts share a platform. |\n| `sync_analytics` | write | Pull an account's latest posts now instead of waiting for the cycle. Debounced 15s. |\n\n## Comments & live reads\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_account_analytics` | read | Live account read: followers, following, post count, reach where exposed. One object, billed on X. |\n| `get_account_media` | read | Recent posts on the account with their metrics and `mediaId`. |\n| `get_post_analytics` | read | Live read of one published post. |\n| `get_comments` | read | Comments on ONE known post, read live. For working through everything that needs answering, use `list_engagement` below. |\n| `reply_to_comment` | write | Reply publicly using PLATFORM ids — threaded on a `commentId`, or on the post itself. |\n\n<Note>\n  `get_*_analytics` query the platform **live**: current value, one object, and\n  a real cost on metered platforms. For anything a dashboard would show — a\n  ranking, a trend, a comparison — the `get_analytics_*` tools above answer in\n  one call and cost nothing.\n</Note>\n\n## Engagement\n\nComments on your posts, replies, and posts that mention you — Instagram,\nFacebook Pages and Threads, in one feed. These take the engagement `_id` from\n`list_engagement`, not a platform comment id.\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_engagement_summary` | read | How much is unanswered, per account. The cheap first call — start here. |\n| `list_engagement` | read | The feed, most recent first. Defaults to what still needs an answer, each row carrying the post it sits under. |\n| `sync_engagement` | write | Pull from the platforms — after an outage, or to reach an older post via `postId`. Webhooks already deliver new comments. |\n| `reply_to_engagement` | write | Publish a PUBLIC reply, signed by the account. Marks the item handled. |\n| `hide_comment` | write | Hide a comment or bring it back. Reversible, author not notified — prefer it to deleting. |\n| `mark_engagement_handled` | write | Clear something that needs no answer. |\n| `delete_comment` | write | Permanently delete a comment on your post. Irreversible. |\n\n<Warning>\n  `reply_to_engagement` and `reply_to_comment` publish **publicly**, under the\n  post, signed by the connected account — they are not DMs, and no platform\n  offers an edit. A mention belongs to its author: it can be replied to, never\n  hidden or deleted.\n</Warning>\n\n## Playbooks\n\nReady-made setups installed in one call on a connected Instagram or Facebook\naccount. The catalog is on [plugkit.co/playbooks](https://www.plugkit.co/playbooks/).\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_playbooks` | read | What can be installed: comment-to-DM lead magnet, story lead magnet, waitlist, welcome message. Start here before building a comment automation by hand. |\n| `get_playbook` | read | The full definition: the account slots to fill, every input with its default, and the automations and sequences it will create. Read it before installing. |\n| `install_playbook` | write | Create everything the playbook describes on one account. Live from that moment, real people get real DMs: show the rendered messages first and get an explicit go-ahead. |\n| `list_playbook_installs` | read | What is already installed here, with the automations and sequences each one created and their status. |\n| `remove_playbook_install` | write | Delete what a playbook created. Pass `keepResources` to unlink the install while leaving everything running. |\n\n## Campaigns\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `create_comment_automation` | write | \"Comment a keyword, get a DM\" on one post or every post of the account. |\n| `list_comment_automations` | read | Automations with their status and counters. |\n| `update_comment_automation` | write | Change keywords, message, button, or pause it. |\n| `delete_comment_automation` | write | Remove an automation. |\n| `get_comment_automation_runs` | read | Trigger log — who commented what, and what was sent. |\n| `preview_broadcast_audience` | read | Count the recipients an audience would reach, **before** sending. |\n| `create_broadcast` | write | Draft or schedule one message to a filtered slice of the inbox. |\n| `list_broadcasts` | read | Broadcasts with status and counts. |\n| `send_broadcast` | write | Queue it for sending. Immediate and irreversible for every recipient. |\n| `cancel_broadcast` | write | Put a scheduled broadcast back to draft. |\n| `delete_broadcast` | write | Delete a broadcast. |\n| `get_broadcast_recipients` | read | Per-recipient delivery log, with the reason for each failure. |\n| `create_sequence` | write | Timed follow-ups, triggered by a new conversation, a comment automation, or a story reply. |\n| `list_sequences` | read | Sequences with their steps and status. |\n| `update_sequence` | write | Edit steps, trigger or status. |\n| `delete_sequence` | write | Remove a sequence. |\n| `enroll_in_sequence` | write | Put a conversation into a sequence by hand. |\n| `get_sequence_enrollments` | read | Who is in it, and at which step. |\n\n## Ads (Meta)\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_ad_account` | read | Currency, status and spend of the ad account. Read it before creating anything. |\n| `list_campaigns` / `list_ad_sets` / `list_ads` | read | The hierarchy, at each level. |\n| `create_campaign` | write | Create a campaign. **Paused** unless told otherwise. |\n| `create_ad_set` | write | Audience, budget and schedule. Budgets in minor units (2000 = €20.00). |\n| `create_ad` | write | An ad from an existing creative or an existing Page post. |\n| `update_campaign` / `update_ad_set` / `update_ad` | write | Rename, pause, resume, rebudget. |\n| `boost_post` | write | Promote a published Page post — campaign, ad set and ad in one call. |\n| `get_ads_insights` | read | Spend and performance for the account, a campaign, an ad set or an ad. |\n\n<Warning>\n  Everything is created **paused**, and budgets are in minor units. Setting\n  `status: \"ACTIVE\"` starts spending real money — the assistant should confirm\n  the amount and the audience with you first.\n</Warning>\n\n## Shopping (Instagram)\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_shopping_status` | read | Whether an account can tag products, and if not, exactly what is missing: no permission, an expired one, a shop Meta has not approved, or a shop with no catalog. Tagging needs a second Facebook authorization the user grants themselves, from the account card on the dashboard's Connections page. |\n| `search_shop_products` | read | Search the account's shop catalog by product name or SKU. The ids returned here are the only ones `productTags` accepts, never invent one. A product whose `taggable` is false cannot be tagged, and its Meta review status says why. |\n| `tag_products_on_post` | write | Tag products on an already published post or reel. **Replaces** the existing tags, so send the complete list. At most 20 on a photo, 30 on a reel; coordinates only apply to a photo. Instagram accepts 25 tagged posts per account per 24 hours. |\n\n## Billing\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_subscription` | read | Plan, account quota, and current X spend. |\n| `get_usage` | read | Pay-per-call spend for the month, broken down by operation. |\n| `set_x_billing` | write | Turn X background analytics and inbox sync on or off, per account. |\n\n<Note>\n  `get_usage` exists so an assistant can weigh a cost **before** starting a\n  loop, instead of discovering it on the invoice.\n</Note>\n\n## API keys\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_api_keys` | read | Keys with prefix, last use and revocation state. Never the secret. |\n| `create_api_key` | write | Create a key. The plaintext is returned **once**, and it lands in the conversation — only use it on request. |\n| `revoke_api_key` | write | Revoke immediately, including the key this connection may be running on. |\n\n## Workflows\n\nThree prompts ship with the server. Clients surface them as commands — in Claude\nCode, `/mcp__plugkit__publish`, `/mcp__plugkit__inbox`, `/mcp__plugkit__report`.\n\n<CardGroup cols={3}>\n  <Card title=\"publish\" icon=\"paper-plane\">\n    Publish one message to every eligible account of a profile, after showing you\n    the targets and the final text.\n  </Card>\n\n  <Card title=\"inbox\" icon=\"inbox\">\n    Sync each messaging account, surface unanswered incoming messages, and draft\n    a reply for each.\n  </Card>\n\n  <Card title=\"report\" icon=\"chart-line\">\n    Published posts, failures and per-account analytics over a period, as a table\n    plus three recommendations.\n  </Card>\n</CardGroup>\n\n## Errors\n\nTools return the API's own errors as text rather than throwing, so the assistant\ncan explain and retry. A `404` means the resource doesn't exist **or** doesn't\nbelong to your account — see [Authentication](/authentication).\n\n\n=================== mcp/clients.md\n# Connect a client\n\n> Add the PlugKit MCP server to Claude, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code, or any other MCP client.\n\nEvery client needs the same thing — the server URL:\n\n```\nhttps://api.plugkit.co/mcp\n```\n\nClients that support OAuth (all the ones below) will open a PlugKit consent\nscreen the first time they connect; approve it and you're done. There is no key\nto paste unless you want one.\n\n<Warning>\n  **You add the connector yourself — not from a chat with the assistant.** An\n  assistant asked to install its own connector will decline (it can't vouch for\n  what it would be connecting to), and an API key pasted into a conversation\n  connects nothing while staying in the history forever. Every\n  step below is something you click or run; it takes about a minute. The\n  [dashboard](https://plugkit.co/dashboard/mcp) walks through the same steps one\n  client at a time and tells you when the server has seen your assistant connect.\n</Warning>\n\n## Claude (web, desktop, mobile)\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step title=\"Open the connector settings\">\n    In Claude, open [Settings → Customize → Connectors](https://claude.ai/new#settings/customize-connectors),\n    then **Add → Custom connector**.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Paste the URL\">\n    Name it `PlugKit` and paste `https://api.plugkit.co/mcp`. Leave the advanced\n    OAuth fields empty — PlugKit registers the client automatically.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Connect and approve\">\n    Click **Connect**. Claude sends you to PlugKit, you sign in if needed, pick\n    read-only or full access, and approve.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Check it\">\n    Ask Claude *\"what PlugKit accounts do I have?\"* — it should call `whoami`\n    and list your profiles and connected accounts.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\n<Note>\n  Custom connectors are available on Free, Pro, Max, Team and Enterprise plans;\n  free accounts are limited to one custom connector.\n</Note>\n\n## ChatGPT\n\nChatGPT takes the same URL — but a custom MCP server is a *developer-mode\nplugin* there, and the switch is not where most people look for it.\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step title=\"Turn developer mode on\">\n    In ChatGPT: **Settings → Security and login → Developer mode**. Not under\n    Apps or Connectors. Without it, the **+** button in the next step does not\n    exist.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Open the plugins screen\">\n    Go to [chatgpt.com/plugins](https://chatgpt.com/plugins), click the **+** in\n    the top right, then **Create app**. A *New plugin* dialog opens on a big\n    drag-and-drop box — ignore the box and click **Create an MCP app**, at the\n    bottom left of that dialog.\n\n    Both other doors are dead ends: **Create plugin** builds a whole plugin\n    package and never asks for a server URL, and **Upload plugin** wants a `.zip`\n    you don't have. *Create app → Create an MCP app* is the only path that asks\n    for one.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Fill in the connection\">\n    Name it `PlugKit`, give it any description, paste\n    `https://api.plugkit.co/mcp` as the MCP server URL, and pick **OAuth**.\n    Leave client id and secret empty — PlugKit registers ChatGPT itself.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Approve, then use it\">\n    ChatGPT opens PlugKit: sign in if needed, pick read-only or full access, and\n    approve. In a new chat, open the **+** menu and select **PlugKit**. Ask\n    *\"search my PlugKit account for the conversation about pricing\"* — it should\n    call `search`, then `fetch` on the result.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\n<Note>\n  Developer mode needs the ChatGPT **web** app and a paid plan (Plus, Pro,\n  Business, Enterprise or Edu). It is not available on the free plan.\n</Note>\n\n<Note>\n  ChatGPT expects every connector to expose two tools with fixed names, `search`\n  and `fetch` — that is how it looks things up and cites them. PlugKit serves\n  both, over everything the account holds: posts, conversations (by contact\n  **and** by what was said in them), accounts, media, automations, sequences,\n  broadcasts and playbooks. They are read-only, and every client gets them, not\n  just ChatGPT.\n</Note>\n\n## Claude Code\n\n```bash theme={null}\nclaude mcp add --transport http --scope user plugkit https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\n```\n\nThen run `/mcp` inside Claude Code and choose **Authenticate** — it opens the\nPlugKit consent screen in your browser. `--scope user` makes the server\navailable in every project; drop it to keep PlugKit to the current folder.\n\nWith an API key instead of OAuth:\n\n```bash theme={null}\nclaude mcp add --transport http --scope user plugkit https://api.plugkit.co/mcp \\\n  --header \"Authorization: Bearer sk_your_key\"\n```\n\nThe three PlugKit workflows show up as slash commands:\n`/mcp__plugkit__publish`, `/mcp__plugkit__inbox`, `/mcp__plugkit__report`.\n\n## Cursor\n\n<Card title=\"Add PlugKit to Cursor\" icon=\"arrow-pointer\" href=\"cursor://anysphere.cursor-deeplink/mcp/install?name=plugkit&config=eyJ0eXBlIjoiaHR0cCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vYXBpLnBsdWdraXQuY28vbWNwIn0%3D\">\n  Opens Cursor and asks you to confirm the server.\n</Card>\n\nOr by hand — **Settings → MCP → Add new MCP server**, or edit\n`~/.cursor/mcp.json`:\n\n```json ~/.cursor/mcp.json theme={null}\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCursor shows a **Needs login** state next to the server — click it to run the\nOAuth flow.\n\n## VS Code\n\n<Card title=\"Add PlugKit to VS Code\" icon=\"code\" href=\"vscode:mcp/install?%7B%22name%22%3A%22plugkit%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22http%22%2C%22url%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fapi.plugkit.co%2Fmcp%22%7D\">\n  Opens VS Code and asks you to confirm the server.\n</Card>\n\nOr from the command line:\n\n```bash theme={null}\ncode --add-mcp '{\"name\":\"plugkit\",\"type\":\"http\",\"url\":\"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"}'\n```\n\nOr by hand in `.vscode/mcp.json` (workspace) / your user `mcp.json`:\n\n```json .vscode/mcp.json theme={null}\n{\n  \"servers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Any other client\n\nThe server speaks plain MCP over Streamable HTTP, so anything that can add a\nremote server works — Windsurf, Zed, LibreChat, Goose, your own agent. The\ncanonical config shape:\n\n```json theme={null}\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf the client doesn't do OAuth, add the header:\n\n```json theme={null}\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\",\n      \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer sk_your_key\" }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### From your own code\n\nAny MCP SDK works. With the TypeScript SDK:\n\n```ts theme={null}\nimport { Client } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js\";\nimport { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/streamableHttp.js\";\n\nconst client = new Client({ name: \"my-agent\", version: \"1.0.0\" });\nawait client.connect(\n  new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(new URL(\"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"), {\n    requestInit: {\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.PLUGKIT_API_KEY}` },\n    },\n  }),\n);\n\nconst { tools } = await client.listTools();\n```\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n<AccordionGroup>\n  <Accordion title=\"My assistant says it doesn't know PlugKit, or refuses to install it\">\n    Expected, and not a fault on either side: PlugKit is a *custom* connector, so\n    it appears in no assistant's built-in list, and an assistant asked to install\n    its own connector will decline — it has no way to vouch for what it would be\n    connecting to. Follow the steps for your client above; the tools appear on\n    their own once the server is added.\n\n    If it asks you to confirm the service is legitimate before you connect, our\n    only addresses are **plugkit.co**, **api.plugkit.co** and **docs.plugkit.co**.\n    Once connected, this is a useful thing to paste to give it its bearings:\n\n    ```\n    I use PlugKit (https://plugkit.co) to manage my social accounts. Its MCP\n    server is https://api.plugkit.co/mcp and its docs are at\n    https://docs.plugkit.co/mcp/overview. I have already added it as a\n    connector — please use its tools, starting with whoami.\n    ```\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"Someone gave me an API key — where do I paste it?\">\n    Nowhere, for Claude, Claude Code, Cursor and VS Code: they sign in instead,\n    which is both easier and safer. A key is only for an app that can't open a\n    sign-in window, or for your own code — it goes in a config file or an\n    environment variable, never in a chat. A key carries full access and doesn't\n    expire, so if one has been through a conversation, revoke it under **API\n    keys** in the dashboard and create another.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"The client asks for a command to run, or an SSE URL\">\n    PlugKit is a **remote** server: nothing to install, no `npx` command, no\n    local proxy. Choose the client's HTTP / Streamable HTTP option and give it\n    `https://api.plugkit.co/mcp`. Legacy SSE is not supported.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"The client says 401 / unauthorized\">\n    The server is reachable but the token isn't valid. With OAuth, disconnect and\n    reconnect the server so it runs the consent flow again. With an API key,\n    check the header is exactly `Authorization: Bearer sk_…` and that the key\n    hasn't been revoked in the dashboard.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"Connected, but the tools do nothing useful\">\n    Ask the assistant to call `whoami` first. Almost every tool needs a\n    `profileId` or an `accountId`, and `whoami` is what hands those out along\n    with what each platform supports.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"Only read tools show up\">\n    The token was granted `mcp:read`. Write tools are not registered at all for a\n    read-only session — reconnect and grant full access if you want the assistant\n    to publish and reply.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"The client tries GET or SSE and fails\">\n    The server is stateless: it answers `POST /mcp` and returns JSON. `GET` and\n    `DELETE` return `405`. Configure the server as **HTTP / Streamable HTTP**,\n    never as legacy **SSE**.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"A one-click button does nothing\">\n    The `cursor://` and `vscode:` links need the app installed on the same\n    machine as the browser, and the browser may ask you to allow opening an\n    external application. If it stays silent, fall back to the JSON config above.\n  </Accordion>\n</AccordionGroup>\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"70a70b6a-0075-445e-80ca-223aeeea29ab","timestamp":"2026-10-03T08:51:15.182Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=================== mcp/tools.md\n# Tools & workflows\n\n> The 103 tools and 3 ready-made workflows exposed by the PlugKit MCP server.\n\nEvery tool maps to the REST API you already know — same vocabulary, same ids.\nTools marked **write** are hidden entirely from a session granted `mcp:read`.\n\n<Note>\n  Start with `whoami`. Nearly every other tool needs a `profileId` or an\n  `accountId`, and `whoami` returns both, plus what each platform supports.\n</Note>\n\n## Search\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `search` | read | One keyword across everything the account holds: posts, conversations (by contact **and** by what was said inside them), accounts, profiles, media, comment→DM rules, sequences, broadcasts, playbooks. Returns short results with an id and a link. |\n| `fetch` | read | Read one result in full from its id — a conversation with its recent messages, a post with its per-platform targets and errors, a published post with its metrics. |\n\n<Note>\n  These two names are fixed by OpenAI: ChatGPT connectors look for `search` and\n  `fetch` to look things up and cite them. Every client gets them all the same —\n  nothing else searches inside message text.\n</Note>\n\n## Profiles\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `whoami` | read | Authenticated identity, profiles, connected accounts, and per-platform capabilities. The entry point. |\n| `list_profiles` | read | Profiles with their account and post counts. |\n| `get_profile` | read | One profile by id. |\n| `create_profile` | write | Create a profile (a workspace grouping the accounts of one brand or client). |\n| `update_profile` | write | Rename, recolour, re-describe, or set as default. |\n| `delete_profile` | write | Delete a profile **and** its accounts, posts and conversations. Irreversible. |\n\n## Accounts & connections\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_accounts` | read | Connected accounts, filterable by profile, platform or status. |\n| `get_account` | read | One account (the platform token is never exposed). |\n| `get_connect_url` | write | Generate the OAuth link for any platform. The assistant hands it to you — it can't follow it itself. |\n| `connect_telegram` | write | Attach a Telegram bot from its BotFather token (validated before storage). |\n| `connect_account_manually` | write | Attach an account from credentials you already hold. Advanced. |\n| `disconnect_account` | write | Disconnect and wipe the token. Posts and conversations are kept. |\n| `subscribe_instagram_webhooks` | write | Re-subscribe an Instagram account to Meta webhooks so DMs arrive in real time. |\n| `list_message_templates` | read | WhatsApp templates the business has had approved — the only thing that sends past the 24h window. |\n| `get_publish_options` | read | TikTok only: what that creator's account allows right now — the privacy levels it accepts, and which of comment / duet / stitch its own settings already disable. Read it before `create_post`; TikTok refuses any other value. |\n| `subscribe_facebook_webhooks` | write | Re-apply the current webhook fields to a connected Facebook Page, including `message_echoes`, which surfaces replies written from the Meta inbox. For Pages connected before those fields existed. No re-authorization needed. |\n\n## Posts\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_posts` | read | Posts with their targets and status, filterable and searchable. |\n| `get_post` | read | One post: content, status, and per target the live URL or the error. |\n| `create_post` | write | Create a post for several accounts at once — draft, scheduled (`scheduledFor`) or immediate (`publishNow`). |\n| `update_post` | write | Edit or reschedule a draft/scheduled post. Published posts are frozen. |\n| `publish_post` | write | Publish now: a draft, a scheduled post brought forward, or a retry (already-published targets are skipped, so no duplicates). |\n| `delete_post` | write | Delete on PlugKit and cancel the schedule. Does not unpublish what's already live. |\n| `unpublish_post` | write | Take a post back off the platform. The opposite of `delete_post`, which only removes it from PlugKit and leaves it live. Threads can undo a publication, Instagram and TikTok cannot. Permanent: ask first. |\n| `upload_media` | write | Re-host a file from a URL you already have, and return the PlugKit URL. |\n| `create_media_upload` | write | Get a one-shot upload link — the assistant sends the bytes itself, or hands you the browser page when it has no shell. Any size, no third-party file host. |\n| `check_media_upload` | read | Confirm the file actually landed before publishing. |\n| `list_media` | read | The account's media library — start here when a post needs a photo or a video, and pass the ids to `create_post` as `mediaIds`. |\n| `delete_media` | write | Remove a file from the library. Refused while an unpublished post still uses it. |\n| `open_media_uploader` | read | Show a drop zone in the conversation, so files on the person's machine reach you without leaving the chat. Several at once, and it stays open for more. Use it instead of `create_media_upload` when you cannot run shell commands. |\n\n<Info>\n  WhatsApp and Telegram don't do posts — only messages. `whoami` returns the\n  capability matrix so the assistant can filter targets itself.\n</Info>\n\n## Messaging\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_conversations` | read | The unified inbox across Instagram, Facebook, WhatsApp and Telegram. |\n| `get_conversation` | read | One conversation (contact, account, platform). |\n| `list_messages` | read | History of a conversation with direction and delivery status. |\n| `list_instagram_threads` | read | Raw Instagram threads with participant IGSIDs — to find a contact the inbox doesn't know yet. |\n| `send_dm` | write | DM someone by name, @handle or phone number — no conversation id. Returns the matches instead of sending when several contacts fit. |\n| `send_message` | write | Reply in an existing conversation. Immediate and irreversible. |\n| `create_conversation` | write | Open a thread with a contact identified by their platform id. |\n| `sync_conversations` | write | Pull conversations and messages from the platform API when the inbox looks stale. |\n| `send_direct_message` | write | Low-level escape hatch: send straight to a platform identifier, storing nothing in the inbox. |\n\n## Analytics\n\nReads the collected store — history, comparisons, every account at once, no\nplatform call. See [Analytics](/guides/analytics).\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_analytics_summary` | read | Headline numbers over a window, each with its change vs the previous period. Start here. |\n| `get_top_posts` | read | Posts ranked by any metric, including ones published outside PlugKit. |\n| `get_analytics_timeseries` | read | One metric by day, week or month, optionally split by platform. |\n| `get_best_times_to_post` | read | Weekday × hour engagement grid — feed it into `scheduledFor` instead of guessing. |\n| `get_follower_stats` | read | Follower history and growth per account. |\n| `get_analytics_by_account` | read | One row per connected account over the window, including the accounts that published nothing. Use it whenever two accounts share a platform. |\n| `sync_analytics` | write | Pull an account's latest posts now instead of waiting for the cycle. Debounced 15s. |\n\n## Comments & live reads\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_account_analytics` | read | Live account read: followers, following, post count, reach where exposed. One object, billed on X. |\n| `get_account_media` | read | Recent posts on the account with their metrics and `mediaId`. |\n| `get_post_analytics` | read | Live read of one published post. |\n| `get_comments` | read | Comments on ONE known post, read live. For working through everything that needs answering, use `list_engagement` below. |\n| `reply_to_comment` | write | Reply publicly using PLATFORM ids — threaded on a `commentId`, or on the post itself. |\n\n<Note>\n  `get_*_analytics` query the platform **live**: current value, one object, and\n  a real cost on metered platforms. For anything a dashboard would show — a\n  ranking, a trend, a comparison — the `get_analytics_*` tools above answer in\n  one call and cost nothing.\n</Note>\n\n## Engagement\n\nComments on your posts, replies, and posts that mention you — Instagram,\nFacebook Pages and Threads, in one feed. These take the engagement `_id` from\n`list_engagement`, not a platform comment id.\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_engagement_summary` | read | How much is unanswered, per account. The cheap first call — start here. |\n| `list_engagement` | read | The feed, most recent first. Defaults to what still needs an answer, each row carrying the post it sits under. |\n| `sync_engagement` | write | Pull from the platforms — after an outage, or to reach an older post via `postId`. Webhooks already deliver new comments. |\n| `reply_to_engagement` | write | Publish a PUBLIC reply, signed by the account. Marks the item handled. |\n| `hide_comment` | write | Hide a comment or bring it back. Reversible, author not notified — prefer it to deleting. |\n| `mark_engagement_handled` | write | Clear something that needs no answer. |\n| `delete_comment` | write | Permanently delete a comment on your post. Irreversible. |\n\n<Warning>\n  `reply_to_engagement` and `reply_to_comment` publish **publicly**, under the\n  post, signed by the connected account — they are not DMs, and no platform\n  offers an edit. A mention belongs to its author: it can be replied to, never\n  hidden or deleted.\n</Warning>\n\n## Playbooks\n\nReady-made setups installed in one call on a connected Instagram or Facebook\naccount. The catalog is on [plugkit.co/playbooks](https://www.plugkit.co/playbooks/).\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_playbooks` | read | What can be installed: comment-to-DM lead magnet, story lead magnet, waitlist, welcome message. Start here before building a comment automation by hand. |\n| `get_playbook` | read | The full definition: the account slots to fill, every input with its default, and the automations and sequences it will create. Read it before installing. |\n| `install_playbook` | write | Create everything the playbook describes on one account. Live from that moment, real people get real DMs: show the rendered messages first and get an explicit go-ahead. |\n| `list_playbook_installs` | read | What is already installed here, with the automations and sequences each one created and their status. |\n| `remove_playbook_install` | write | Delete what a playbook created. Pass `keepResources` to unlink the install while leaving everything running. |\n\n## Campaigns\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `create_comment_automation` | write | \"Comment a keyword, get a DM\" on one post or every post of the account. |\n| `list_comment_automations` | read | Automations with their status and counters. |\n| `update_comment_automation` | write | Change keywords, message, button, or pause it. |\n| `delete_comment_automation` | write | Remove an automation. |\n| `get_comment_automation_runs` | read | Trigger log — who commented what, and what was sent. |\n| `preview_broadcast_audience` | read | Count the recipients an audience would reach, **before** sending. |\n| `create_broadcast` | write | Draft or schedule one message to a filtered slice of the inbox. |\n| `list_broadcasts` | read | Broadcasts with status and counts. |\n| `send_broadcast` | write | Queue it for sending. Immediate and irreversible for every recipient. |\n| `cancel_broadcast` | write | Put a scheduled broadcast back to draft. |\n| `delete_broadcast` | write | Delete a broadcast. |\n| `get_broadcast_recipients` | read | Per-recipient delivery log, with the reason for each failure. |\n| `create_sequence` | write | Timed follow-ups, triggered by a new conversation, a comment automation, or a story reply. |\n| `list_sequences` | read | Sequences with their steps and status. |\n| `update_sequence` | write | Edit steps, trigger or status. |\n| `delete_sequence` | write | Remove a sequence. |\n| `enroll_in_sequence` | write | Put a conversation into a sequence by hand. |\n| `get_sequence_enrollments` | read | Who is in it, and at which step. |\n\n## Ads (Meta)\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_ad_account` | read | Currency, status and spend of the ad account. Read it before creating anything. |\n| `list_campaigns` / `list_ad_sets` / `list_ads` | read | The hierarchy, at each level. |\n| `create_campaign` | write | Create a campaign. **Paused** unless told otherwise. |\n| `create_ad_set` | write | Audience, budget and schedule. Budgets in minor units (2000 = €20.00). |\n| `create_ad` | write | An ad from an existing creative or an existing Page post. |\n| `update_campaign` / `update_ad_set` / `update_ad` | write | Rename, pause, resume, rebudget. |\n| `boost_post` | write | Promote a published Page post — campaign, ad set and ad in one call. |\n| `get_ads_insights` | read | Spend and performance for the account, a campaign, an ad set or an ad. |\n\n<Warning>\n  Everything is created **paused**, and budgets are in minor units. Setting\n  `status: \"ACTIVE\"` starts spending real money — the assistant should confirm\n  the amount and the audience with you first.\n</Warning>\n\n## Shopping (Instagram)\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_shopping_status` | read | Whether an account can tag products, and if not, exactly what is missing: no permission, an expired one, a shop Meta has not approved, or a shop with no catalog. Tagging needs a second Facebook authorization the user grants themselves, from the account card on the dashboard's Connections page. |\n| `search_shop_products` | read | Search the account's shop catalog by product name or SKU. The ids returned here are the only ones `productTags` accepts, never invent one. A product whose `taggable` is false cannot be tagged, and its Meta review status says why. |\n| `tag_products_on_post` | write | Tag products on an already published post or reel. **Replaces** the existing tags, so send the complete list. At most 20 on a photo, 30 on a reel; coordinates only apply to a photo. Instagram accepts 25 tagged posts per account per 24 hours. |\n\n## Billing\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `get_subscription` | read | Plan, account quota, and current X spend. |\n| `get_usage` | read | Pay-per-call spend for the month, broken down by operation. |\n| `set_x_billing` | write | Turn X background analytics and inbox sync on or off, per account. |\n\n<Note>\n  `get_usage` exists so an assistant can weigh a cost **before** starting a\n  loop, instead of discovering it on the invoice.\n</Note>\n\n## API keys\n\n| Tool | | What it does |\n| - | - | - |\n| `list_api_keys` | read | Keys with prefix, last use and revocation state. Never the secret. |\n| `create_api_key` | write | Create a key. The plaintext is returned **once**, and it lands in the conversation — only use it on request. |\n| `revoke_api_key` | write | Revoke immediately, including the key this connection may be running on. |\n\n## Workflows\n\nThree prompts ship with the server. Clients surface them as commands — in Claude\nCode, `/mcp__plugkit__publish`, `/mcp__plugkit__inbox`, `/mcp__plugkit__report`.\n\n<CardGroup cols={3}>\n  <Card title=\"publish\" icon=\"paper-plane\">\n    Publish one message to every eligible account of a profile, after showing you\n    the targets and the final text.\n  </Card>\n\n  <Card title=\"inbox\" icon=\"inbox\">\n    Sync each messaging account, surface unanswered incoming messages, and draft\n    a reply for each.\n  </Card>\n\n  <Card title=\"report\" icon=\"chart-line\">\n    Published posts, failures and per-account analytics over a period, as a table\n    plus three recommendations.\n  </Card>\n</CardGroup>\n\n## Errors\n\nTools return the API's own errors as text rather than throwing, so the assistant\ncan explain and retry. A `404` means the resource doesn't exist **or** doesn't\nbelong to your account — see [Authentication](/authentication).\n\n\n=================== mcp/clients.md\n# Connect a client\n\n> Add the PlugKit MCP server to Claude, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code, or any other MCP client.\n\nEvery client needs the same thing — the server URL:\n\n```\nhttps://api.plugkit.co/mcp\n```\n\nClients that support OAuth (all the ones below) will open a PlugKit consent\nscreen the first time they connect; approve it and you're done. There is no key\nto paste unless you want one.\n\n<Warning>\n  **You add the connector yourself — not from a chat with the assistant.** An\n  assistant asked to install its own connector will decline (it can't vouch for\n  what it would be connecting to), and an API key pasted into a conversation\n  connects nothing while staying in the history forever. Every\n  step below is something you click or run; it takes about a minute. The\n  [dashboard](https://plugkit.co/dashboard/mcp) walks through the same steps one\n  client at a time and tells you when the server has seen your assistant connect.\n</Warning>\n\n## Claude (web, desktop, mobile)\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step title=\"Open the connector settings\">\n    In Claude, open [Settings → Customize → Connectors](https://claude.ai/new#settings/customize-connectors),\n    then **Add → Custom connector**.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Paste the URL\">\n    Name it `PlugKit` and paste `https://api.plugkit.co/mcp`. Leave the advanced\n    OAuth fields empty — PlugKit registers the client automatically.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Connect and approve\">\n    Click **Connect**. Claude sends you to PlugKit, you sign in if needed, pick\n    read-only or full access, and approve.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Check it\">\n    Ask Claude *\"what PlugKit accounts do I have?\"* — it should call `whoami`\n    and list your profiles and connected accounts.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\n<Note>\n  Custom connectors are available on Free, Pro, Max, Team and Enterprise plans;\n  free accounts are limited to one custom connector.\n</Note>\n\n## ChatGPT\n\nChatGPT takes the same URL — but a custom MCP server is a *developer-mode\nplugin* there, and the switch is not where most people look for it.\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step title=\"Turn developer mode on\">\n    In ChatGPT: **Settings → Security and login → Developer mode**. Not under\n    Apps or Connectors. Without it, the **+** button in the next step does not\n    exist.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Open the plugins screen\">\n    Go to [chatgpt.com/plugins](https://chatgpt.com/plugins), click the **+** in\n    the top right, then **Create app**. A *New plugin* dialog opens on a big\n    drag-and-drop box — ignore the box and click **Create an MCP app**, at the\n    bottom left of that dialog.\n\n    Both other doors are dead ends: **Create plugin** builds a whole plugin\n    package and never asks for a server URL, and **Upload plugin** wants a `.zip`\n    you don't have. *Create app → Create an MCP app* is the only path that asks\n    for one.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Fill in the connection\">\n    Name it `PlugKit`, give it any description, paste\n    `https://api.plugkit.co/mcp` as the MCP server URL, and pick **OAuth**.\n    Leave client id and secret empty — PlugKit registers ChatGPT itself.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step title=\"Approve, then use it\">\n    ChatGPT opens PlugKit: sign in if needed, pick read-only or full access, and\n    approve. In a new chat, open the **+** menu and select **PlugKit**. Ask\n    *\"search my PlugKit account for the conversation about pricing\"* — it should\n    call `search`, then `fetch` on the result.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\n<Note>\n  Developer mode needs the ChatGPT **web** app and a paid plan (Plus, Pro,\n  Business, Enterprise or Edu). It is not available on the free plan.\n</Note>\n\n<Note>\n  ChatGPT expects every connector to expose two tools with fixed names, `search`\n  and `fetch` — that is how it looks things up and cites them. PlugKit serves\n  both, over everything the account holds: posts, conversations (by contact\n  **and** by what was said in them), accounts, media, automations, sequences,\n  broadcasts and playbooks. They are read-only, and every client gets them, not\n  just ChatGPT.\n</Note>\n\n## Claude Code\n\n```bash theme={null}\nclaude mcp add --transport http --scope user plugkit https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\n```\n\nThen run `/mcp` inside Claude Code and choose **Authenticate** — it opens the\nPlugKit consent screen in your browser. `--scope user` makes the server\navailable in every project; drop it to keep PlugKit to the current folder.\n\nWith an API key instead of OAuth:\n\n```bash theme={null}\nclaude mcp add --transport http --scope user plugkit https://api.plugkit.co/mcp \\\n  --header \"Authorization: Bearer sk_your_key\"\n```\n\nThe three PlugKit workflows show up as slash commands:\n`/mcp__plugkit__publish`, `/mcp__plugkit__inbox`, `/mcp__plugkit__report`.\n\n## Cursor\n\n<Card title=\"Add PlugKit to Cursor\" icon=\"arrow-pointer\" href=\"cursor://anysphere.cursor-deeplink/mcp/install?name=plugkit&config=eyJ0eXBlIjoiaHR0cCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vYXBpLnBsdWdraXQuY28vbWNwIn0%3D\">\n  Opens Cursor and asks you to confirm the server.\n</Card>\n\nOr by hand — **Settings → MCP → Add new MCP server**, or edit\n`~/.cursor/mcp.json`:\n\n```json ~/.cursor/mcp.json theme={null}\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCursor shows a **Needs login** state next to the server — click it to run the\nOAuth flow.\n\n## VS Code\n\n<Card title=\"Add PlugKit to VS Code\" icon=\"code\" href=\"vscode:mcp/install?%7B%22name%22%3A%22plugkit%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22http%22%2C%22url%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fapi.plugkit.co%2Fmcp%22%7D\">\n  Opens VS Code and asks you to confirm the server.\n</Card>\n\nOr from the command line:\n\n```bash theme={null}\ncode --add-mcp '{\"name\":\"plugkit\",\"type\":\"http\",\"url\":\"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"}'\n```\n\nOr by hand in `.vscode/mcp.json` (workspace) / your user `mcp.json`:\n\n```json .vscode/mcp.json theme={null}\n{\n  \"servers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Any other client\n\nThe server speaks plain MCP over Streamable HTTP, so anything that can add a\nremote server works — Windsurf, Zed, LibreChat, Goose, your own agent. The\ncanonical config shape:\n\n```json theme={null}\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf the client doesn't do OAuth, add the header:\n\n```json theme={null}\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"plugkit\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\",\n      \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer sk_your_key\" }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### From your own code\n\nAny MCP SDK works. With the TypeScript SDK:\n\n```ts theme={null}\nimport { Client } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js\";\nimport { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/streamableHttp.js\";\n\nconst client = new Client({ name: \"my-agent\", version: \"1.0.0\" });\nawait client.connect(\n  new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(new URL(\"https://api.plugkit.co/mcp\"), {\n    requestInit: {\n      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.PLUGKIT_API_KEY}` },\n    },\n  }),\n);\n\nconst { tools } = await client.listTools();\n```\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n<AccordionGroup>\n  <Accordion title=\"My assistant says it doesn't know PlugKit, or refuses to install it\">\n    Expected, and not a fault on either side: PlugKit is a *custom* connector, so\n    it appears in no assistant's built-in list, and an assistant asked to install\n    its own connector will decline — it has no way to vouch for what it would be\n    connecting to. Follow the steps for your client above; the tools appear on\n    their own once the server is added.\n\n    If it asks you to confirm the service is legitimate before you connect, our\n    only addresses are **plugkit.co**, **api.plugkit.co** and **docs.plugkit.co**.\n    Once connected, this is a useful thing to paste to give it its bearings:\n\n    ```\n    I use PlugKit (https://plugkit.co) to manage my social accounts. Its MCP\n    server is https://api.plugkit.co/mcp and its docs are at\n    https://docs.plugkit.co/mcp/overview. I have already added it as a\n    connector — please use its tools, starting with whoami.\n    ```\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"Someone gave me an API key — where do I paste it?\">\n    Nowhere, for Claude, Claude Code, Cursor and VS Code: they sign in instead,\n    which is both easier and safer. A key is only for an app that can't open a\n    sign-in window, or for your own code — it goes in a config file or an\n    environment variable, never in a chat. A key carries full access and doesn't\n    expire, so if one has been through a conversation, revoke it under **API\n    keys** in the dashboard and create another.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"The client asks for a command to run, or an SSE URL\">\n    PlugKit is a **remote** server: nothing to install, no `npx` command, no\n    local proxy. Choose the client's HTTP / Streamable HTTP option and give it\n    `https://api.plugkit.co/mcp`. Legacy SSE is not supported.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"The client says 401 / unauthorized\">\n    The server is reachable but the token isn't valid. With OAuth, disconnect and\n    reconnect the server so it runs the consent flow again. With an API key,\n    check the header is exactly `Authorization: Bearer sk_…` and that the key\n    hasn't been revoked in the dashboard.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"Connected, but the tools do nothing useful\">\n    Ask the assistant to call `whoami` first. Almost every tool needs a\n    `profileId` or an `accountId`, and `whoami` is what hands those out along\n    with what each platform supports.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"Only read tools show up\">\n    The token was granted `mcp:read`. Write tools are not registered at all for a\n    read-only session — reconnect and grant full access if you want the assistant\n    to publish and reply.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"The client tries GET or SSE and fails\">\n    The server is stateless: it answers `POST /mcp` and returns JSON. `GET` and\n    `DELETE` return `405`. Configure the server as **HTTP / Streamable HTTP**,\n    never as legacy **SSE**.\n  </Accordion>\n\n  <Accordion title=\"A one-click button does nothing\">\n    The `cursor://` and `vscode:` links need the app installed on the same\n    machine as the browser, and the browser may ask you to allow opening an\n    external application. If it stays silent, fall back to the JSON config above.\n  </Accordion>\n</AccordionGroup>","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"3a67df62-bc59-4d65-87ad-fa2ecc5529c4","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":1,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ca054bdb-cdaa-454f-89b4-fe1a99894c8f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"00ffe75e-952c-45fc-88a9-6f1bb05adeb3","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"main"}
